// ===== AI-DERIVED CATEGORY DEFINITIONS =====
var CAT_WHY = {
mono: {name:"Monotheism & Theology of God",why:"Scripture's most foundational claim. Deut 6:4 (Shema), Isa 45:5, Mark 12:29, 1 Cor 8:6, James 2:19, 1 Enoch 84:2-3. Confirmed across Torah, Prophets, Yeshua, Paul, and additional texts. Any deviation from strict monotheism (polytheism, modalism that erases distinctions, deification of humans) represents the most fundamental scriptural violation. This category also measures knowledge of and honor for His personal name — Yahweh (יהוה / YHVH). Exodus 3:15: 'This is my name forever, and this is my memorial to all generations.' The Name appears 6,828 times in scripture and was replaced — not translated — with the title LORD across Hebrew → Greek → Latin → English. A denomination that has never taught Yahweh's personal name, that consistently substitutes a title for the Name He declared permanent, has a deficiency in this most foundational category: knowing who God actually is."},
messiah: {name:"Identity of Messiah",why:"Who Yeshua is determines everything. Prophesied: Isa 7:14, 9:6-7, 53, Dan 7:13-14, Mic 5:2, Zech 12:10. Affirmed: John 1:1,14, Matt 16:16, Phil 2:6-11, Heb 1:3, Rev 19:16. He must be recognized as divine Messiah, atoning sacrifice, resurrected Lord, AND living Torah (John 1:14 — the Word/Torah made flesh). Reducing Him to mere teacher, political figure, or one-of-many fails the scriptural witness."},
canon: {name:"Scripture as Final Authority",why:"Deut 4:2 and 12:32 forbid adding to or subtracting from God's commands. Prov 30:5-6 warns against adding. Rev 22:18-19 closes the canon with the same warning. Matt 15:3-9 — Yeshua condemned elevating tradition over scripture. 2 Tim 3:16 — all scripture is God-breathed. Any denomination that places councils, prophets, popes, governing bodies, or modern revelations ABOVE or EQUAL to scripture violates this multi-witness principle."},
sabbath: {name:"Seventh-Day Sabbath",why:"Established at creation (Gen 2:2-3) — before sin, before Israel, before Sinai. Codified in the Ten Commandments (Ex 20:8-11). Called eternal covenant (Ex 31:16-17, olam). Kept by patriarchs pre-Sinai (Jubilees 2:17-33). Prophets affirm it (Isa 56:2-6, 58:13-14). Yeshua kept it (Luke 4:16) and called Himself Lord OF it (Mark 2:28). Millennial confirmation: Isa 66:23 — ALL flesh, Sabbath to Sabbath, forever. This category has the MAXIMUM number of scriptural witnesses of any practice. Sunday observance has zero scriptural mandate — the earliest record of a Sunday gathering is Justin Martyr's First Apology (~150 AD), and Constantine's decree (321 AD) made Sunday rest Roman civil law; it enforced the practice, it did not invent it.<br><br><strong>Texts set against this, and what the Word shows.</strong> Col 2:16-17 lists 'a Sabbath day' among shadows whose substance belongs to Christ; Rom 14:5-6 lets one person esteem one day above another; Gal 4:10 faults 'observing days'; Acts 20:7, 1 Cor 16:2 and Rev 1:10 show first-day gatherings. What the text shows: Col 2 is about outsiders judging how believers keep these days, and it calls the Sabbath a shadow of something still coming, which does not abolish it; Acts 20:7 records a meeting and no command to move the day, while Yeshua (Luke 4:16) and Paul (Acts 13:14, 17:2, 18:4) kept the seventh day; Rom 14 deals with disputable matters, and the creation pattern and Ex 31:16-17 put the Sabbath outside that category. On olam: the Hebrew uses the same word for the priesthood. Ex 40:15 gives Aaron's sons a priesthood of olam, Num 25:13 gives Phinehas a covenant of olam priesthood, and Ps 110:4 swears a priest le'olam after Melchizedek, so the word alone fixes no single arrangement. Heb 7:12 says the Aaronic priesthood changed, Heb 7:13-14 says why (a priest from Judah), and Ps 110:4 had already named the priest who would stand. The Sabbath is anchored on its own: Gen 2:2-3 at creation, Ex 31:16-17 ('a covenant of olam... a sign... for in six days Yahweh made the heavens and the earth'), Heb 4:9 ('there remains a sabbatismos for the people of God'), Isa 56:6-7 (foreigners who join themselves to Yahweh keep the Sabbath and hold fast His covenant) and Isa 66:23, where all flesh keeps it in the new heavens and new earth. Nothing in Scripture moves it. <sup class='rn-mark' data-rn='R10,R9' title='Research'>👁️</sup>"},
feasts: {name:"Appointed Times / Biblical Feasts",why:"Leviticus 23 — God calls them 'MY appointed times' (not Israel's). Each declared olam (eternal): Passover (Lev 23:14), Shavuot (23:21), Yom Kippur (23:31), Sukkot (23:41). Yeshua kept all of them. Paul kept them (Acts 20:16, 1 Cor 5:7-8). Millennial proof: Zech 14:16-19 — Sukkot MANDATORY for all nations, plague for refusal. Pre-Sinai observance: Jubilees 6:17-22 (Shavuot), 16:20-31 (Sukkot). Christmas, Easter, and other substitutions have no scriptural mandate. Their link to older pagan festivals (Saturnalia, spring fertility rites) is a matter of history, and the Scripture test does not depend on it: Deuteronomy 12:30-32 forbids worshipping Yahweh the way the nations worship their gods, and Leviticus 23 names the days He appointed.<br><br><strong>Texts set against this, and what the Word shows.</strong> Col 2:16-17 names 'a festival or a new moon'; Gal 4:10 faults observing 'days and months and seasons and years'; Rom 14:5 treats days as a matter of conviction. What the text shows: Gal 4:3-5 puts 'we' under 'the elements of the world': Jews under the law (4:5) and Gentiles under 'them which by nature are no gods' (4:8). Gal 4:21 and 5:2-4 name the error: desiring to be 'under law' (hypo nomon) and being justified in it, a return to the yoke of bondage (5:1). The verb in 4:10, paratereo ('observe closely'), is used everywhere else in the New Testament for watching someone in order to accuse or to kill (Mark 3:2; Luke 6:7; 14:1; 20:20; Acts 9:24), and it is not the word Paul uses for his own keeping: 'let us keep the feast' (heortazomen, 1 Cor 5:8) and hurrying to Jerusalem for Pentecost (Acts 20:16). Paul was not 'under law' as a standing (1 Cor 9:20), yet he 'walked orderly, keeping the law' (Acts 21:24), and Zech 14:16-19 makes Sukkot mandatory for the nations. Gal 4:10 condemns observing days as the bondage of earning or holding a standing before God; it does not forbid keeping Yahweh's appointed times as His children. <sup class='rn-mark' data-rn='R6,R10' title='Research'>👁️</sup>"},
torah: {name:"Torah as Living Instruction",why:"The central question of this entire analysis. Yeshua: 'Do not think I came to abolish Torah' (Matt 5:17-19) — heaven and earth duration. Paul: 'Do we nullify Torah through faith? Never! We establish it' (Rom 3:31). Paul: 'Torah is holy, righteous, and good' (Rom 7:12). John: 'Sin IS Torah-lessness' (1 John 3:4). New Covenant: same Torah on hearts (Jer 31:33). Millennium: Torah from Zion (Isa 2:3). Rev 14:12 — saints keep commandments AND faith of Yeshua. The original Hebrew word Torah means 'instruction/teaching' not 'law' — it is parental guidance, not a legal burden.<br><br><strong>Texts set against this, and what the Word shows.</strong> Rom 10:4 can be read 'Christ is the end of the law'; Rom 6:14 and 7:4-6 speak of being 'not under law' and having 'died to the law'; Gal 3:24-25 says we are 'no longer under a guardian'; 2 Cor 3:7-11 calls the Sinai ministry one of death that is being set aside; Heb 8:13 calls the first covenant 'obsolete.' What the text shows: telos in Rom 10:4 is limited by its own words 'for righteousness' (Rom 3:31 and 9:31-10:6 point the same way), the guardian of Gal 3 is the law's custody and curse (Gal 3:13, 23-25), and Heb 8:13 is about the Levitical priesthood and sacrifices (Heb 7:12; 8:3-5), not the whole of Torah. 'Not under law' and 'died to the law' are answered in Rom 3:19, 6:15-16 and 7:1-12. In the Greek, the verb Paul uses for the release in 7:2 and 7:6 (katargeo) has the believer as its object ('we were released from the law'), never the law, and in 3:31 the same verb is denied of the law: 'Do we then make void the law through faith? May it never be; rather we establish the law.' 'Under law' (hypo nomon) is a standing, not a way of walking: Paul was 'not himself under law' (1 Cor 9:20) and yet 'walked orderly, keeping the law' (Acts 21:24), while the law stays 'holy, and just, and good' (7:12) and Paul 'delights in the law of God' (7:22). Gal 3:19 says the law 'was added because of transgressions until the seed should come'; the commandments themselves go back before Sinai (Gen 26:5: Abraham kept 'my charge, my commandments, my statutes, and my laws'), and what Messiah ended was the curse and the custody (Gal 3:13, 23-25). 2 Cor 3 renders inoperative the glory, the ministry of death and the veil (3:7, 11, 13, 14), and says the Spirit writes on 'tables of the heart' (3:3) what Jer 31:33 calls 'my Torah' and Heb 8:10 'my laws.' Jer 31:33, Matt 5:17-19 and the Millennial passages settle it. <sup class='rn-mark' data-rn='R14,R2,R4,R5,R8,R9' title='Research'>👁️</sup>"},
diet: {name:"Dietary Obedience (Clean/Unclean)",why:"Leviticus 11 and Deuteronomy 14 establish clear categories. Peter — years after the resurrection — had 'never eaten anything unclean' (Acts 10:14) and interpreted his vision as being about PEOPLE not food (Acts 10:28). The Greek of Mark 7:19 ('purifying all foods') is in the earliest manuscripts; what is contested is grammatical, and the whole passage is about unwashed hands (Mark 7:2-5), with 'foods' meaning things Torah already counts as food (see Key Argument 8). Maccabean martyrs died rather than eat pork (1 Macc 1:62-63). Millennial proof: Isa 66:17 — God judges pig-eaters at His return. No NT verse explicitly abolishes Leviticus 11 food categories.<br><br><strong>Texts set against this, and what the Word shows.</strong> Mark 7:19 ('purifying all foods'), Acts 10:9-16 (the sheet vision), Rom 14:14 and 14:20 ('nothing is unclean in itself'), 1 Tim 4:3-5 ('every creature of God is good') and Col 2:16 are the standard arguments that the food laws ended. What the text shows: Mark 7 is about unwashed hands and about what counts as food in the first place (see Key Argument 8); Peter reads Acts 10 as being about people (10:28) and had still never eaten anything unclean years after the resurrection (10:14); Rom 14 deals with eating herbs or 'all things,' flesh and wine (14:2, 21), not Leviticus 11; 1 Tim 4:3-5 speaks of what is 'received with thanksgiving' and 'consecrated by the word of God,' which presupposes food that Torah already defined. Matt 15:20 reports the same teaching and ends, 'to eat with unwashed hands defiles not a man,' with no 'purging all meats' clause, so the subject is hands. In Rom 14:14 the word is <em>koinos</em> (common), not <em>akathartos</em> (unclean), the two words Peter keeps apart in Acts 10:14. <em>Koinos</em> is Mark 7's own word: the objection there is to eating with <em>koinais</em> hands, 'that is, unwashed' (Mark 7:2, 5), and Heb 9:13 uses the same word family for the ritually defiled. The foods in dispute in Rom 14:2 and 14:21 are vegetables, meat and wine, which Leviticus 11 does not prohibit as such. <sup class='rn-mark' data-rn='R7' title='Research'>👁️</sup>"},
grace: {name:"Grace-Faith-Obedience Balance",why:"Scripture teaches BOTH grace AND obedience as inseparable partners. Grace saves: Eph 2:8-9, Rom 3:24. But grace does not license sin: Rom 6:1-2,15. Faith without works is dead: James 2:17,26. The end-time saints are defined by BOTH: 'those who keep the commandments of God AND the faith of Yeshua' (Rev 14:12). Hab 2:4 — the righteous live by faith. Matt 7:21 — not everyone who says Lord Lord enters the kingdom, but those who DO the Father's will. Legalism (earning salvation) and antinomianism (grace without standards) are both errors."},
purity: {name:"Examination of Worship Origins",why:"Torah: 'Do not learn the way of the nations' (Jer 10:2). 'Do not inquire about their gods saying how did they worship' (Deut 12:29-32). Ezekiel 8 — God showed Ezekiel pagan practices IN the Temple as abominations. Rev 2:14,20 — Yeshua condemns Balaam doctrine and Jezebel (both involve mixing pagan practice with worship of God). 2 Cor 6:14-17 — what fellowship has light with darkness? 1 Macc 1:41-50 — Antiochus forced syncretism, faithful Jews resisted to death. This category scores how thoroughly a tradition examines the historical origins of its practices against the scriptural standard — inviting self-examination rather than labeling specific observances as condemned."},
justice: {name:"Justice, Mercy & Compassion",why:"Mic 6:8 — what does YHWH require? Do justice, love mercy, walk humbly. Isa 1:17 — learn to do good, seek justice, correct oppression. Deut 10:17-19 — God executes justice for orphan and widow, loves the stranger. Lev 19:9-10 — gleaning laws for the poor. Matt 25:31-46 — nations judged by how they treated hungry, thirsty, stranger, naked, sick, imprisoned. James 1:27 — pure religion = visiting orphans and widows. Sirach 4:1-5 — do not reject the plea of the afflicted. This is not optional — Yeshua makes it a basis of judgment."},
moral: {name:"Moral Holiness & Personal Conduct",why:"Lev 18-20 — comprehensive moral code including sexual ethics, honest business, no occult. 1 Cor 6:9-11 — lists of behaviors that exclude from the kingdom. Gal 5:19-21 — works of the flesh vs fruit of the Spirit. Heb 13:4 — marriage honorable, sexual immorality judged. Matt 5:27-28 — Yeshua brought Torah's moral demands to their heart-level fullness, a depth Torah already held (Lev 19:17-18; Gen 4:6-7; the prophets' marriage-covenant lens in Hosea, Jer 3, Ezek 16). He unveiled it rather than adding to it. 1 Cor 6:19-20 — body is temple. 1 Enoch 8:1-3 — Watchers taught forbidden practices (sorcery, cosmetics for seduction, weaponry). Deut 18:10-12 — occult practices are abomination."},
doctrine: {name:"Sound Doctrine / False Teaching Rejection",why:"Deut 13:1-5 — test prophets against Torah. Matt 7:15 — beware false prophets in sheep's clothing. Matt 24:4-5,11,24 — many will be deceived. Rev 2:2 — commended for testing false apostles. Rev 2:6,14-15,20 — condemned for tolerating Nicolaitan, Balaam, Jezebel doctrines. 1 John 4:1 — test every spirit. 1 Thess 5:21 — test everything, hold what is good. 2 Tim 4:3-4 — time will come when they won't endure sound doctrine. Acts 17:11 — Bereans tested Paul against scripture. A denomination that cannot be questioned against scripture has already failed this test."},
endure: {name:"Endurance & Persecution Readiness",why:"Matt 24:13 — whoever endures to the END will be saved. Rev 2:10 — be faithful unto death. Rev 3:10 — kept the word of My perseverance. Heb 10:36 — you need endurance to receive what is promised. James 1:12 — blessed is the one who endures trial. 2 Macc 7 — mother and seven sons chose death over Torah violation. 1 Macc 2:19-22 — Mattathias refused to compromise. The faith is not designed to be comfortable — it is designed to be durable. Denominations that promise prosperity, ease, or cultural acceptance may not produce believers who can endure."},
prophrev: {name:"Prophetic & Revelation Alignment",why:"COMBINED CATEGORY: Millennial prophecy + Revelation 2-3 profile. Both measure the same thing: will this denomination need to change when Messiah returns? Millennial: Sabbath for all flesh (Isa 66:23), feasts mandatory (Zech 14:16-19), Torah from Zion (Isa 2:3), dietary judgment (Isa 66:17), temple worship (Ezek 40-48). Rev 2-3: Yeshua commends endurance, truth, rejecting false teaching, keeping His word. Condemns lukewarmness, dead works, tolerance of syncretism, loss of first love. A denomination whose current practice matches the Millennial standard scores high. One that will need wholesale reform scores low."},
love: {name:"Love & Foundation",why:"Yeshua declared the two greatest commandments: love Yahweh your God with all your heart, soul, and mind — and love your neighbor as yourself. Then He added: 'On these two commandments hang ALL the law and prophets' (Matt 22:37-40). This is not a category among equals. It is the root from which every other category is supposed to grow. Deuteronomy 6:5 (the Shema) comes BEFORE the commandments that follow — the commandments are the response to love, not the path to earn it. Hosea 6:6: 'I desire steadfast love and not sacrifice.' The prophets repeat it: Micah 6:8 reduces the entire requirement to justice, mercy, and humble walk with God. John defines love as walking in His commandments (2 John 1:6) — love and obedience are inseparable. Paul says the entire Torah is fulfilled in 'love your neighbor as yourself' (Gal 5:14). This category scores whether a tradition's entire practice is explicitly grounded in love as its operating foundation — whether Torah is kept as a love response to God (Deut 6:5 → then the commandments) rather than as performance, tradition, or fear. A high score here does not replace the other 16 categories — it asks WHY the others are practiced. The warning of Ephesus stands over every tradition: 'You have left your first love.' Correct doctrine without the fire of love loses the right to the lampstand (Rev 2:4-5)."},
spirit: {name:"Walking in the Spirit / Ruach HaKodesh",why:"The New Covenant promise is explicitly pneumatological. Ezekiel 36:26-27 — 'I will put My Spirit within you and cause you to walk in My statutes, and you will keep My judgments and do them.' The Spirit's declared purpose is covenant faithfulness — not replacement of the covenant. This is the same movement described in Jeremiah 31:33: Torah written on hearts. Galatians 5:22-23 names the Spirit's fruit: love, joy, peace, longsuffering, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control — and adds 'against such there is no law.' The Spirit's fruit and Torah's standard point the same direction. Romans 8:4 — the righteous requirement of Torah is fulfilled in those who walk according to the Spirit, not the flesh. John 16:13 — the Spirit of truth guides into all truth, not away from it. This category measures whether a tradition correctly understands the Spirit as the agent Ezekiel described — the one who empowers covenant-faithfulness — rather than reducing the Spirit to emotional experience, sacramental mediation, tongues as primary evidence, or a force rather than a person. A tradition that elevates Spirit-experience over Spirit-fruit misidentifies what the Spirit came to do. 1 Corinthians 6:19 — your body is the Temple of the Ruach HaKodesh — the same holiness standards that governed the physical Temple now govern the person the Spirit inhabits."},
humility: {name:"Humility & Beatitude Alignment",why:"Isaiah 66:2 — 'To this one I will look: to him who is humble and contrite of spirit, and who trembles at My word.' God names the person He pays attention to. Isaiah 57:15 — 'I dwell in the high and holy place — and also with him who is of a contrite and lowly spirit, to revive the spirit of the lowly.' God dwells with the broken, not the impressive. Psalm 51:17 — 'A broken and contrite heart, O God, You will not despise.' The one sacrifice that is never rejected. Matthew 5:3-12 — the Beatitudes are not aspirational ideals. Yeshua is describing the people the kingdom belongs to: poor in spirit, mourning, meek, hungry for righteousness, merciful, pure in heart, peacemaker, persecuted for righteousness. James 4:6 (quoting Proverbs 3:34) — 'God opposes the proud but gives grace to the humble.' Two outcomes. No middle ground. Luke 1:51-53 — Mary's Magnificat: 'He has scattered the proud... brought down the mighty from their thrones and exalted the humble... filled the hungry with good things and sent the rich away empty.' God's active inversion of human status systems. This category measures whether a tradition produces Beatitude people — humble, contrite, meek, mercy-full — or whether it produces the opposite: pride about doctrinal correctness, spiritual elitism, platform Christianity, or the prosperity inversion of the Beatitudes. A tradition can be doctrinally accurate across all other categories and still fail here if it cultivates the posture James 4:6 says God opposes."}
};

