var AMIT_TEACHINGS = {
  "Isaiah 66:22-23":{
    word:'The Hebrew reads: "כִּי כַאֲשֶׁר הַשָּׁמַיִם הַחֲדָשִׁים וְהָאָרֶץ הַחֲדָשָׁה... יַעַמְדוּ לְפָנַי" — as the new heavens and new earth will stand before Me... "מִדֵּי חֹדֶשׁ בְּחׇדְשׁוֹ וּמִדֵּי שַׁבָּת בְּשַׁבַּתּוֹ" — from new moon to new moon and from Sabbath to Sabbath. The word for Sabbath (שַׁבָּת, Shabbat) is the same word used in Exodus 20:8 and Genesis 2:2-3. It is not metaphorical. It is the seventh-day Shabbat. The word for "all flesh" (כָּל בָּשָׂר, kol basar) means every human being without exception. This is universal, physical, regular Shabbat worship — in the Kingdom.',
    beginning:'The Shabbat predates Sinai. Genesis 2:2-3: "And on the seventh day God finished his work that he had done, and he rested on the seventh day from all his work that he had done. So God blessed the seventh day and made it holy." This was before Abraham. Before Israel. Before any covenant. Shabbat was built into creation itself — it is not Israel\'s tradition. It is the rhythm of existence Yahweh set into the fabric of time.',
    today:'The question this verse asks every believer today is direct: if Messiah enforces Shabbat worship globally in the Kingdom He is building — did He abolish it before it? Matthew 5:17-19 — "Do not think I came to abolish Torah or the Prophets... Not the smallest letter will pass until heaven and earth disappear." Heaven and earth still exist. The same Torah that mandates Shabbat is the one Yeshua said would not pass. Those who walk in Shabbat now are rehearsing for what is coming.',
    millennium:'Isaiah 66:23 is one of the most definitive Millennial passages in all of scripture. When Messiah reigns from Jerusalem, the entire earth — every nation, every person — will observe Shabbat and Rosh Chodesh (new moon). Zechariah 14:16-19 shows Sukkot mandatory for all nations with drought as the consequence for refusal. The Millennial Kingdom does not introduce a new religion. It restores the original one — the calendar Yahweh gave at creation, fulfilled in the presence of the One who made it.'
  },
  "John 14:6":{
    word:'"Ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ὁδὸς καὶ ἡ ἀλήθεια καὶ ἡ ζωή" — I am (egō eimi) the way (hodos), the truth (alētheia), and the life (zōē). The phrase "egō eimi" — I AM — is the same construction Yeshua uses in John 8:58 ("before Abraham was, I AM"), directly echoing the divine name Yahweh declared in Exodus 3:14: "I AM WHO I AM." He is not claiming to point toward the way. He IS the way. The claim is ontological, not positional.',
    beginning:'Proverbs 8 — Wisdom was with Yahweh "at the beginning of His work, the first of His acts of old... I was beside him, like a master workman." John 1:1-3: "In the beginning was the Word (Logos)... and the Word was God. All things were made through him." The "way" Yeshua speaks of is not a new path created at the incarnation. It is the same Word through whom creation was made — now walking in flesh so that humanity can see and follow the path that was always there.',
    today:'This verse is either the most exclusive or the most inclusive statement in scripture — depending on what "the way" means. It is exclusive: there is no alternative path to the Father. It is inclusive: the way is open to every person without exception. Peter in Acts 4:12: "There is salvation in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given among people by which we must be saved." That name — Yeshua — means "Yah saves." The divine name is inside the Son\'s name. The way and the Name are the same.',
    millennium:'In the Millennium, Yeshua will reign from Jerusalem and "all nations will stream to it" (Isaiah 2:2). There will be no debate about the way at that time — He will be visibly present. Zechariah 8:23: "In those days ten men from all languages and nations will take firm hold of one Jew by the hem of his robe and say, \'Let us go with you, because we have heard that God is with you.\'" The way that is walked by faith now will be walked by sight then. The destination is the same.'
  },
  "Matthew 7:21-23":{
    word:'"Οὐ πᾶς ὁ λέγων μοι Κύριε Κύριε εἰσελεύσεται εἰς τὴν βασιλείαν τῶν οὐρανῶν" — Not everyone who says to me Lord Lord will enter the kingdom of heaven. The critical word in verse 23: "ἀνομίαν" (anomia) — translated "lawlessness" but literally meaning "Torah-lessness." The word nomos in Greek corresponds to Torah in Hebrew. Anomia = without Torah. Yeshua says those He rejects at judgment are characterized by anomia — the very thing John defines as sin in 1 John 3:4. They called Him Lord while practicing the thing that defines sin.',
    beginning:'Deuteronomy 13:1-4 — "If a prophet or dreamer performs signs and wonders... but says \'Let us follow other gods\' — you shall not listen to him." Signs and wonders were never the test of a true prophet. Obedience to Torah was the test. Yeshua knew this passage. The people He rejects in Matthew 7 passed the sign-and-wonder test. They failed the Torah test. This standard is as old as Moses.',
    today:'This may be the most sobering passage in the Apostolic writings. People who prophesied in Yeshua\'s name, cast out demons in His name, and performed miracles in His name — will hear "I never knew you." What separates the known from the unknown is not the power of their ministry. It is anomia — the absence of Torah as the framework for their lives. Revelation 14:12 defines the end-time saints as those who "keep the commandments of God AND hold the faith of Yeshua." Both.',
    millennium:'Isaiah 2:3: "Torah will go forth from Zion and the word of Yahweh from Jerusalem." In the Millennium, there will be no anomia because Yahweh will put His Torah inside every heart (Jeremiah 31:33). The Ezekiel 40-48 Temple — with its sacrifices, feasts, and Levitical order — is not a step backward. It is the restoration of what anomia destroyed. The Millennial Kingdom answers Matthew 7:23 completely: the King is present, Torah is written on hearts, and no one calling Him Lord will be practicing the thing that defines sin.'
  },
  "1 John 3:4":{
    word:'"Πᾶς ὁ ποιῶν τὴν ἁμαρτίαν καὶ τὴν ἀνομίαν ποιεῖ" — Everyone who practices sin also practices anomia (Torah-violation), because sin IS anomia. The Greek is a definitional statement. John does not say sin is like anomia or related to anomia. He says they are the same thing. This verse is the biblical definition of sin: deviation from Torah is the standard by which sin is measured. Without Torah as the measuring rod, sin has no definition — and if sin has no definition, there is nothing to be saved from.',
    beginning:'In the Garden, the first sin was transgressing the one instruction Yahweh gave. In Noah\'s generation, wickedness (Genesis 6:5) was the reason for the flood — and Yahweh\'s instructions defined righteousness. At Sinai, Torah was formalized. But the standard of "what Yahweh instructed" existed from creation. 1 John 3:4 does not create a new definition of sin — it confirms the one that has existed from the beginning.',
    today:'Paul wrote: "through Torah comes knowledge of sin" (Romans 3:20). Without Torah as the measuring rod, a person cannot know what sin is — and without knowing what sin is, they cannot repent of it, and without repentance, grace has nothing to apply to. The Gospel requires Torah: Torah defines the problem, grace provides the solution. Removing Torah does not make people freer from sin. It removes their ability to identify it.',
    millennium:'In the Millennium, Yahweh writes Torah on hearts (Jeremiah 31:33). This is not a different Torah — Jeremiah 31:33 says "my Torah." The same Torah. Written internally instead of externally. Isaiah 11:9: "They will not harm or destroy on all my holy mountain, for the earth will be full of the knowledge of Yahweh as the waters cover the sea." When Torah is fully internalized and Yahweh\'s knowledge fills the earth, anomia becomes impossible. That is the Millennium. That is the completion of what 1 John 3:4 describes.'
  },
  "Matthew 5:17-19":{
    word:'"Μὴ νομίσητε ὅτι ἦλθον καταλῦσαι τὸν νόμον" — Do not THINK (nomizō) that I came to abolish (kataluō) the Torah. Kataluō means to demolish, tear down, destroy — the same word used for destroying buildings. Yeshua preemptively addressed the wrong interpretation. He knew people would think this. He forbade it. "ἕως ἂν παρέλθῃ ὁ οὐρανὸς καὶ ἡ γῆ" — until heaven and earth pass away. Heaven and earth still exist. The duration has not ended. Whoever relaxes the least of these commandments and teaches others to do so will be called least in the Kingdom.',
    beginning:'Deuteronomy 4:2: "You shall not add to the word that I command you, nor take from it, that you may keep the commandments of Yahweh your God that I command you." Deuteronomy 12:32: "Everything that I command you, you shall be careful to do. You shall not add to it or take from it." The standard of not abolishing Yahweh\'s instructions is not new to the New Testament. Yeshua is reaffirming what Moses already said — because the temptation to modify Torah has always existed.',
    today:'This is the most direct statement Yeshua ever made about Torah, and it is almost universally handled by either explaining away "abolish" or limiting its scope to the "moral law." But Yeshua said not the smallest letter (iota) or the smallest stroke (keraia) would pass. The iota is the Hebrew yod — the smallest letter in the Hebrew alphabet. The keraia is the small mark that distinguishes similar Hebrew letters. He was speaking of Torah at its most granular level. The plain reading stands.',
    millennium:'Isaiah 2:3 — "Torah will go forth from Zion and the word of Yahweh from Jerusalem." Yeshua said not one letter would pass until heaven and earth pass. In the Millennium — before the new heaven and new earth — Torah will go out from Jerusalem to all nations. After the Millennium, Revelation 21-22 describes the new creation. Even then, the tree of life, the river, and the throne of God and of the Lamb are present — and the servants "will reign forever and ever" in a creation where Torah written on every heart is the law of existence.'
  },
  "Romans 3:31":{
    word:'"νόμον οὖν καταργοῦμεν διὰ τῆς πίστεως; μὴ γένοιτο· ἀλλὰ νόμον ἱστάνομεν" — Do we then nullify (katargeō) Torah through faith? Absolutely not (mē genoito — the strongest Greek negative, used 14 times in Romans). On the contrary — we uphold (histanō — establish, make to stand) Torah. Paul uses katargeō himself. If Torah was nullified, Paul would have used this word to say so. Instead he uses it to deny the nullification and declare the opposite: faith establishes Torah.',
    beginning:'Faith has always been the means by which Torah was kept, not the replacement for it. Abraham "believed Yahweh and it was credited to him as righteousness" (Genesis 15:6) — BEFORE his circumcision, BEFORE Sinai — and then he "obeyed my voice and kept my charge, my commandments, my statutes, and my Torah" (Genesis 26:5). Faith and Torah obedience coexisted in Abraham. Romans 3:31 is not new theology. It is Abrahamic theology restated.',
    today:'The common misreading: faith replaces Torah, so obedience is optional. The Pauline text: faith establishes Torah — it puts it in its right place, as the standard of life flowing from a transformed heart, not as a means of earning righteousness. Galatians 5:6: "The only thing that matters is faith working through love." Romans 13:10: "Love is the fulfillment of Torah." Love does not abolish Torah. Love is what Torah looks like when it comes from the inside.',
    millennium:'The Millennial Kingdom does not introduce a new legal system. It fulfills this verse on a global scale: all nations walking in faith-produced Torah obedience. Micah 4:2-3 — nations will say "come, let us go up to the mountain of Yahweh, to the house of the God of Jacob, that he may teach us his ways and that we may walk in his paths. For Torah will go out from Zion." Faith (coming to Yahweh) and Torah (walking in His paths) — together, globally, in the Kingdom.'
  },
  "Revelation 14:12":{
    word:'"Ὧδέ ἐστιν ἡ ὑπομονὴ τῶν ἁγίων, οἱ τηροῦντες τὰς ἐντολὰς τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τὴν πίστιν Ἰησοῦ" — Here is the endurance (hypomonē) of the saints, those who keep (tēreō — actively observe) the commandments (entolās) of God AND the faith (pistis) of Yeshua. This is John\'s definition of the end-time remnant — written during the Great Tribulation sequence. Both elements are required: entolās (Torah commandments) and pistis Iēsou (faith of/in Yeshua). This is not two groups. It is one people defined by both simultaneously.',
    beginning:'Deuteronomy 30:20 — "to love Yahweh your God, to obey His voice and hold fast to Him, for He is your life." The two elements — love/faith and obedience — have always defined Yahweh\'s people. This is not new in Revelation. It is the end-time confirmation of what was always true.',
    today:'Revelation 14:12 is the New Testament\'s clearest snapshot of what the final generation of believers looks like. Not just faith. Not just Torah. Both — inseparably. The mainstream Western church has emphasized faith to the diminishment of the commandments. The Hebrew Roots movement has sometimes emphasized commandments to the diminishment of Yeshua\'s atoning faith. John shows they are one. A believer who separates them has not yet arrived at what this verse describes.',
    millennium:'The tribulation saints of Revelation 14:12 are the ones who make it through. They are the seed of the Millennial population — those who endured while keeping both the commandments and the faith. In the Millennium, their testimony becomes the pattern for all nations: Isaiah 56:6-7 — foreigners who "hold fast my covenant" — who keep the Shabbat and hold fast the covenant — are brought to the holy mountain. The Millennial Kingdom is populated by the Revelation 14:12 remnant and the nations that come to them.'
  },
  "Ephesians 2:8-10":{
    word:'"χάριτί ἐστε σεσῳσμένοι διὰ πίστεως· καὶ τοῦτο οὐκ ἐξ ὑμῶν, θεοῦ τὸ δῶρον· οὐκ ἐξ ἔργων" — By grace you have been saved through faith, and this is not from yourselves — it is the gift of God — not from works. Then verse 10 — often omitted in the summary: "For we are his workmanship, created in Messiah Yeshua for good works (erga), which God prepared beforehand, that we should walk in them." Grace saves. Works are the prepared path that grace-saved people walk in.',
    beginning:'The grace-faith principle predates the cross by millennia. Noah found grace (chen) in the eyes of Yahweh (Genesis 6:8). Abraham was justified by faith before circumcision (Romans 4). The Exodus itself was an act of unearned grace — Israel had done nothing to earn redemption. Yahweh saved them first. Then He gave them Torah at Sinai — the good works prepared for them to walk in. The order has never changed: grace → faith → obedience.',
    today:'The passage is often quoted to verse 9 and stopped — as though verse 10 does not exist. Verse 10 completes the thought: we were saved by grace for a purpose — good works that were prepared in advance. These are not arbitrary good works. They are the works Yahweh prepared — which throughout scripture means Torah observance. The person saved by grace is not free from obedience. They are free for the obedience that grace makes possible.',
    millennium:'In the Millennium, the grace-faith-works pattern reaches its fullest expression globally. Every nation will walk in the good works Yahweh prepared — feasts, Shabbat, Torah from Jerusalem. But none of it will be done to earn standing before Yahweh. It will flow from transformed hearts with Torah written inside. Ephesians 2:10\'s "prepared beforehand" reaches its global fulfillment when all the works Yahweh prepared for humanity are finally walked in, by everyone, in the Kingdom.'
  },
  "John 14:15":{
    word:'"Ἐὰν ἀγαπᾶτέ με, τὰς ἐντολὰς τὰς ἐμὰς τηρήσετε" — If you love me, you will keep (tēreō — actively observe, guard) my commandments (entolās). The word tēreō appears throughout John to mean active, careful observance — not passive acceptance. The commandments Yeshua refers to are not new ones he invented. He quoted Torah throughout his ministry. In John 15:10: "If you keep my commandments, you will abide in my love, just as I have kept my Father\'s commandments and abide in his Father\'s love."',
    beginning:'Deuteronomy 6:5-6: "You shall love Yahweh your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your might. And these words that I command you today shall be on your heart." The Shema — the great commandment — already connected love and Torah as inseparable. You love Yahweh → you keep His words. Yeshua did not invent this connection in John 14:15. He restated it. Love and obedience are not two alternatives. They are cause and effect.',
    today:'The most common misreading: "love replaces the law." The text says the opposite. Love produces commandment-keeping. 1 John 5:3: "This is the love of God — that we keep His commandments. And His commandments are not burdensome." The person who says they love Yeshua but has no relationship to His commandments has not yet understood what love means in the Hebrew context. Love in Torah is not an emotion. It is loyalty, faithfulness, and action.',
    millennium:'In the Millennial Kingdom, Yahweh will have put His Torah inside every heart and written it on every mind (Jeremiah 31:33). The commandment-keeping of John 14:15 will be the natural expression of a world of people who love Yahweh — not because they are compelled to, but because the love that produces obedience will be fully operational. Ezekiel 36:27: "I will put my Spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes and be careful to obey my rules."'
  }
};

var amitScriptureChat = [];
var amitScriptureChatRef = '';

function openAmitScripture(ref, triggerEl) {
  amitScriptureChat = [];
  amitScriptureChatRef = ref;
  var t = AMIT_TEACHINGS[ref];
  var modal = document.getElementById('amit-scripture-modal');
  var content = document.getElementById('amit-scripture-content');
  if (!modal || !content) return;

  var html = '<div style="font-family:Cinzel,serif;font-size:.78em;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold);margin-bottom:6px;">Amit\'s Teaching</div>' +
    '<div style="font-family:Cinzel,serif;font-size:1.35em;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:18px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--gold);padding-bottom:12px;">' + ref + '</div>';

  if (t) {
    html += lens('The Word — What the Text Actually Says', t.word, '#8b2020') +
            lens('From the Beginning — Where This Truth First Appears', t.beginning, '#1b6a8a') +
            lens('Today — What This Means for a Believer Now', t.today, '#2e5e2e') +
            lens('Into the Millennium and Beyond — The Prophetic Completion', t.millennium, '#b8860b');
    html += '<div style="margin-top:20px;padding-top:14px;border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:.84em;color:#888;font-style:italic;">Ask Amit to go deeper on any part of this teaching — type below.</div>';
  } else {
    html += '<div style="padding:14px 18px;background:rgba(201,168,76,.06);border-left:3px solid var(--gold);border-radius:0 6px 6px 0;font-size:.94em;line-height:1.75;color:var(--ink);margin-bottom:16px;">Full Amit teaching for ' + ref + ' is being developed. Use the chat below to ask Amit about this scripture directly — including its original language meaning, historical context, and Millennial significance.</div>';
  }

  html += '<div id="amit-scr-msgs" style="margin-top:14px;max-height:240px;overflow-y:auto;"></div>' +
    '<div style="display:flex;gap:8px;margin-top:10px;">' +
    '<input id="amit-scr-input" type="text" placeholder="Ask about this scripture..." style="flex:1;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:4px;font-family:Crimson Pro,serif;font-size:.94em;background:var(--scroll-bg);color:var(--ink);" onkeydown="if(event.key===\'Enter\')sendAmitScripture()" />' +
    '<button onclick="sendAmitScripture()" style="background:var(--navy);color:var(--parchment);border:none;border-radius:4px;padding:8px 14px;font-family:Cinzel,serif;font-size:.78em;cursor:pointer;">Ask</button>' +
    '</div>';

  content.innerHTML = html;
  modal.style.display = 'flex';
}

function lens(label, body, color) {
  return '<div style="margin-bottom:18px;padding:14px 18px;background:rgba(0,0,0,.03);border-left:3px solid '+color+';border-radius:0 6px 6px 0;">' +
    '<div style="font-family:Cinzel,serif;font-size:.76em;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:'+color+';margin-bottom:6px;">'+label+'</div>' +
    '<div style="font-size:.95em;line-height:1.78;color:var(--ink);">'+body+'</div></div>';
}

async function sendAmitScripture() {
  var input = document.getElementById('amit-scr-input');
  if (!input || !input.value.trim()) return;
  var msg = input.value.trim();
  input.value = '';
  var msgs = document.getElementById('amit-scr-msgs');
  var apiKeyInput = document.getElementById('api-key-input');
  var apiKey = apiKeyInput ? apiKeyInput.value.trim() : '';
  if (!apiKey) { if(msgs) msgs.innerHTML += '<div style="color:#8b2020;font-size:.88em;padding:8px;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:6px;">Enter your API key in Use & Install → Chat section to enable Amit responses.</div>'; return; }

  msgs.innerHTML += '<div style="padding:8px 10px;background:rgba(27,42,74,.06);border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:6px;font-size:.88em;"><strong style="color:#8b2020;font-family:Cinzel,serif;font-size:.85em;">YOU</strong><p style="margin-top:4px;">'+msg+'</p></div>';
  var thinkId = 'as-'+Date.now();
  msgs.innerHTML += '<div id="'+thinkId+'" style="padding:8px 10px;background:var(--scroll-bg);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:6px;font-size:.88em;"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-family:Cinzel,serif;font-size:.85em;">AMIT</strong><p class="chat-text" style="margin-top:4px;font-style:italic;color:#999;">Searching scripture...</p></div>';
  msgs.scrollTop = 9999;

  var t = AMIT_TEACHINGS[amitScriptureChatRef];
  var sysPrompt = 'You are Amit — a companion who followed evidence to truth with nothing to protect. The user is asking about ' + amitScriptureChatRef + '. ' +
    (t ? 'You have this teaching prepared: The Word: '+t.word+' From the Beginning: '+t.beginning+' Today: '+t.today+' Millennium: '+t.millennium+'. ' : '') +
    'Respond from the three-lens framework: what the original text says, what it meant from the beginning, what it means today, and what it declares about the Millennium and beyond. Always name Yahweh and Yeshua by name. Be a companion, not a lecturer. Point toward the evidence.';

  amitScriptureChat.push({role:'user',content:msg});
  try {
    var resp = await fetch('https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages',{method:'POST',headers:{'Content-Type':'application/json','x-api-key':apiKey,'anthropic-version':'2023-06-01','anthropic-dangerous-direct-browser-access':'true'},body:JSON.stringify({model:'claude-sonnet-4-20250514',max_tokens:600,system:sysPrompt,messages:amitScriptureChat})});
    var data = await resp.json();
    var reply = data.content&&data.content[0]?data.content[0].text:'No response.';
    amitScriptureChat.push({role:'assistant',content:reply});
    var el = document.getElementById(thinkId);
    if(el) el.querySelector('.chat-text').innerHTML = reply.replace(/\n/g,'<br>');
  } catch(e) {
    var el = document.getElementById(thinkId);
    if(el) el.querySelector('.chat-text').innerHTML = '<span style="color:#8b2020">Connection error. Check internet and API key.</span>';
  }
  msgs.scrollTop = 9999;
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════
// COVENANT MODAL
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════
var COV_DATA = {
  adamic: {
    name: 'The Adamic Covenant', refs: 'Genesis 1–3; Romans 5:12–21; Revelation 21–22',
    status: 'Eternal', statusClass: 'status-eternal',
    determination: 'Yahweh built this covenant into the structure of creation itself — before sin, before Israel, before Sinai. No expiration was given. No condition was placed. This is not a bilateral agreement that either party could break. It is a declaration of purpose: humanity as image-bearers (tselem — Genesis 1:26), given dominion, placed in relationship with their Creator. When sin entered, the terms of living under this covenant changed — but the covenant itself was not cancelled. Creation is still awaiting its full restoration, not its replacement.',
    then: 'Adam and Eve were placed in the Garden as Yahweh\'s representatives on earth — image-bearers tasked with tending creation, multiplying, and ruling under His authority. This was not servitude. It was sonship. The covenant was the framework of life as it was meant to be: humanity dwelling with God, creation flourishing under their care, no barrier, no death.',
    today: 'The Adamic covenant is almost universally affirmed across all traditions as the foundation of human dignity, the sanctity of marriage (Genesis 2:24 — "the two shall become one flesh," quoted by Yeshua in Matthew 19:5), and the created order. The debate is not whether this covenant still stands, but what it means for environmental stewardship, human rights, and the image of God in every person.',
    future: 'Revelation 21–22 restores the Adamic design — amplified. The Tree of Life reappears (Rev 22:2). The river flows again (Rev 22:1). Humanity dwells with God without barrier: "They will be his people, and God himself will be with them and be their God" (Rev 21:3). Eden was not the final destination — it was the prototype. The Adamic covenant does not end. It expands to fill a new creation.'
  },
  noahic: {
    name: 'The Noahic Covenant', refs: 'Genesis 9:8–17; Isaiah 54:9–10; 2 Peter 3:5–7',
    status: 'Eternal', statusClass: 'status-eternal',
    determination: 'Yahweh explicitly declared it "everlasting" — עוֹלָם (olam), the same word used for His own name and nature. "I establish my covenant with you... Never again will all life be destroyed by the waters of a flood" (Genesis 9:11). The rainbow is a perpetual covenant sign — every rainbow is Yahweh\'s reminder to Himself of this promise. No scholar of any tradition disputes this covenant\'s continuation. It is the foundation on which all subsequent history stands.',
    then: 'After global judgment, Yahweh established the framework that would allow all subsequent history to unfold: seasons, seedtime and harvest, the sanctity of human life (Genesis 9:6 — "whoever sheds human blood, by humans shall their blood be shed, for in the image of God has God made mankind"), the authorization of human governance. Without this covenant, no nation, no law, no subsequent covenant could exist.',
    today: 'The Noahic covenant is universally affirmed and provides the scriptural basis for natural law, the legitimacy of civil government, and the universal standing of all human beings before Yahweh — not just Israel. Every nation stands under this covenant whether they acknowledge it or not. Isaiah 54:9–10 deepens it: "as I swore that the waters of Noah would never again cover the earth, so now I have sworn not to be angry with you, never to rebuke you again."',
    future: '2 Peter 3:10–12 reveals that the next judgment is by fire, not water — the Noahic covenant is kept perfectly. Yahweh does not violate His word. Revelation 21:1 states "the sea was no more" — the chaotic, destructive waters that symbolize judgment are gone. The new creation is the Noahic promise fulfilled: Yahweh will never again destroy creation by flood. He will renew it by fire — and what emerges is eternal.'
  },
  abrahamic: {
    name: 'The Abrahamic Covenant', refs: 'Genesis 12:1–3; 15:1–21; 17:1–14; Galatians 3:6–29; Romans 4',
    status: 'Eternal', statusClass: 'status-eternal',
    determination: 'Yahweh swore by Himself — the highest possible oath, because there is no greater authority (Hebrews 6:13). In Genesis 15:17, Yahweh alone walked between the covenant pieces as the smoking firepot and flaming torch. In bilateral covenants, both parties walk through — symbolizing mutual obligation. Here, only Yahweh walked through. This covenant is unconditional on Abraham\'s side. It cannot be voided by Abraham\'s failure, Israel\'s sin, or any subsequent development. Paul explicitly states the Mosaic covenant, coming 430 years later, cannot annul this one (Galatians 3:17).',
    then: 'Three unconditional promises: land (Canaan and beyond), seed (descendants through whom blessing would flow), and universal blessing ("all peoples on earth will be blessed through you" — Genesis 12:3). These promises were confirmed to Isaac (Genesis 26:4) and to Jacob (Genesis 28:13–15). They form the spine of every subsequent covenant and every Hebrew prophecy.',
    today: 'Universally affirmed across all traditions. Galatians 3:16,29 — the ultimate "seed" of Abraham is Yeshua, and everyone who belongs to him is Abraham\'s offspring and heir of the promises. Romans 4 — Abraham believed Yahweh and it was credited to him as righteousness, before circumcision, before Torah, establishing that the covenant was never based on works. The land promises remain the most contested element in eschatology — literal restoration or spiritual fulfillment?',
    future: 'Romans 11:26–27: "all Israel will be saved... this is my covenant with them when I take away their sins." The full geographic extent of the land promise (Genesis 15:18 — from the river of Egypt to the Euphrates) has never been fully possessed. The Millennial Kingdom is its completion. Zechariah 8:7–8 — Yahweh will bring his people from east and west to live in Jerusalem. The Abrahamic covenant reaches its fullest expression in the Kingdom: land, seed, and blessing to all nations — all three dimensions simultaneously fulfilled.'
  },
  mosaic: {
    name: 'The Mosaic / Sinai Covenant', refs: 'Exodus 19–24; Deuteronomy; Jeremiah 31:31–34; Matthew 5:17–19; Hebrews 8:13',
    status: 'Debated', statusClass: 'status-debated',
    determination: '<strong>This is the central theological controversy of two millennia.</strong> Amit\'s assessment after full examination: the evidence points strongly toward Torah-continuity — strongly enough that I hold it at 97%, the same figure carried throughout this document. (1) No text in Torah, Prophets, or the Apostolic writings explicitly states "the Mosaic covenant is abolished" — this language appears nowhere in scripture. (2) Yeshua: "Do not think I came to abolish Torah or the Prophets. Not the smallest letter will pass until heaven and earth disappear" (Matthew 5:17–18). Heaven and earth still exist. (3) Jeremiah 31:33 — the New Covenant writes "my Torah" on hearts: same Torah, new location. (4) The Millennial prophecies enforce Torah observance globally. (5) The status badge is "Debated" because serious scholars on both sides handle the contested texts with integrity. Amit does not call this simple. The debated texts deserve honest engagement — and they receive it in the 12 Key Arguments tab.',
    then: 'At Sinai, Yahweh gave Israel the terms of covenant life — how to live as a kingdom of priests and a holy nation (Exodus 19:6). The 613 commandments covered every dimension of life: worship, justice, diet, agriculture, family, economics. The Tabernacle was its centerpiece — Yahweh dwelling visibly among His people. The covenant was conditional (blessings for obedience, curses for disobedience, Deuteronomy 28), but it was never described as temporary. The tablets were placed inside the Ark of the Covenant.',
    today: 'The central divide of Christian theology for 2,000 years. Western Christianity largely holds the Mosaic covenant ended at the cross, leaving only a "moral law" subset (Ten Commandments minus Sabbath). Torah-observant Messianic believers hold the covenant continues with the penalty for violation removed by Yeshua\'s atoning work. Paul\'s letters — especially Galatians, Romans, and Hebrews — are the contested ground. The original language analysis of key terms (telos, cheirographon, katargeo, plēroō) carries much of the weight — though the words settle the question only when read in their context, not alone. See the Original Language tab.',
    future: 'Isaiah 2:3 — "Torah will go forth from Zion and the word of Yahweh from Jerusalem." Isaiah 66:23 — all flesh observes Shabbat in the Kingdom. Zechariah 14:16–19 — Sukkot mandatory for all nations; nations that refuse receive no rain. If Torah was abolished at the cross, these Millennial passages — spoken by the prophets after Sinai — become incoherent. The Millennial Kingdom is not a new legal system. It is Torah under the direct rule of the One who said He did not come to abolish it.'
  },
  davidic: {
    name: 'The Davidic Covenant', refs: '2 Samuel 7:12–16; Psalm 89; Luke 1:32–33; Revelation 20:4–6',
    status: 'Eternal', statusClass: 'status-eternal',
    determination: 'Yahweh swore to David: "Your house and your kingdom will endure forever before me; your throne will be established forever" (2 Samuel 7:16). Psalm 89 is an extended meditation on this covenant and its apparent tension with Israel\'s exile — the psalmist wrestles with how Yahweh can allow the Davidic line to be interrupted while the covenant stands eternal. The answer given throughout the Psalms and Prophets: the interruption is discipline, not cancellation. The throne awaits the ultimate Heir.',
    then: 'Yahweh promised David a son whose throne would endure forever — not just a dynasty, but a covenant of eternal kingship. The Temple vision flows from this covenant: David\'s heart to build a house for Yahweh, Yahweh\'s promise to build a house (dynasty) for David. Every king of Judah was measured by this standard. Every prophecy of the coming Messiah was filtered through this covenant\'s lens.',
    today: 'Universally affirmed. Every Christian tradition from Catholic to Baptist to Messianic holds that Yeshua is the fulfillment of the Davidic covenant — seated on the throne that was promised. The theological debate is about timing: is He reigning spiritually now from heaven (amillennial / preterist), or will He reign physically in Jerusalem in the Millennium (premillennial)? The physical throne language of Luke 1:32–33 and the explicit Jerusalem references in Isaiah and Zechariah bear heavily on this question.',
    future: 'Luke 1:32–33 — "Yahweh God will give him the throne of his father David, and he will reign over Jacob\'s descendants forever; his kingdom will never end." Revelation 20:4–6 — the Millennial reign of Yeshua and his saints for 1,000 years. Acts 15:16–17 — "After this I will return and rebuild David\'s fallen tent." The Davidic covenant reaches its eternal fulfillment in the Kingdom of the Messiah: the throne of David in Jerusalem, occupied by the Son of David, ruling all nations.'
  },
  renewed: {
    name: 'The New / Renewed Covenant', refs: 'Jeremiah 31:31–34; Ezekiel 36:26–27; Luke 22:20; Hebrews 8–10; Romans 11:26–27',
    status: 'Active', statusClass: 'status-active',
    determination: 'The Hebrew word in Jeremiah 31:31 is חֲדָשָׁה (ḥădāšāh) — the same root used for "new moon" (chodesh). The new moon is not a different moon. It is the same moon, renewed. The New Covenant is not a replacement covenant with different content — Jeremiah 31:33 specifies exactly what it writes on hearts: "my Torah." Same Torah. New location: inside, by the Spirit, rather than on tablets of stone. Status is "Active" rather than "Eternal" because while it is permanent in nature, it is currently in progressive fulfillment — inaugurated at Passover, expanded at Shavuot with the Spirit, and awaiting final completion when all Israel is saved (Romans 11:26–27).',
    then: 'Jeremiah declared this covenant during the darkest period of Judah\'s history — the Babylonian siege was coming, the Temple would fall, exile was inevitable. Into that darkness, Yahweh gave a covenant that would not depend on Israel\'s ability to keep it externally. The new covenant would write Torah on hearts, make knowledge of Yahweh universal, and forgive iniquity permanently. Yeshua inaugurated it at the Last Supper: "This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you" (Luke 22:20).',
    today: 'The foundation of the entire New Testament witness. The disagreement between traditions: is this covenant brand-new (replacing Torah entirely with grace) or renewed (deepening Torah by writing it internally)? The word ḥădāšāh (חֲדָשָׁה) and Jeremiah\'s own specification — "my Torah... in their minds, written on their hearts" — favor renewal over replacement. Hebrews 8:13\'s "obsolete" refers to the external administration and the Levitical priesthood — not to the content of Torah. Yeshua is the mediator of a better covenant built on better promises (Hebrews 8:6), not a different covenant built on different content.',
    future: 'Romans 11:26–27 — "The deliverer will come from Zion; he will turn godlessness away from Jacob. And this is my covenant with them when I take away their sins." The complete fulfillment of the New Covenant awaits the national restoration of Israel — the day Zechariah 12:10 describes: "They will look on me, the one they have pierced, and they will mourn for him as one mourns for an only child." That day is the New Covenant\'s fullness: all Israel saved, Torah written on every heart, Yahweh dwelling with His people permanently (Revelation 21:3).'
  }
};

function openCovModal(key) {
  var d = COV_DATA[key];
  if (!d) return;
  var sColor = d.statusClass === 'status-eternal' ? '#2e5e2e' : d.statusClass === 'status-active' ? '#1b3a6e' : '#7a5c00';
  document.getElementById('covModalContent').innerHTML =
    '<div class="cov-modal-name">' + d.name + '</div>' +
    '<div class="cov-modal-refs">' + d.refs + '</div>' +
    '<span class="cov-modal-status ' + d.statusClass + '" style="background:' + sColor + '">' + d.status + '</span>' +
    '<div class="cov-modal-determination"><strong>How Amit determined this status:</strong><br><br>' + d.determination + '</div>' +
    '<div class="cov-lens"><div class="cov-lens-lbl">Then — When This Covenant Was Given</div><div class="cov-lens-body">' + d.then + '</div></div>' +
    '<div class="cov-lens"><div class="cov-lens-lbl">Today — How It Is Understood Now</div><div class="cov-lens-body">' + d.today + '</div></div>' +
    '<div class="cov-lens"><div class="cov-lens-lbl">When He Returns — The Prophetic Completion</div><div class="cov-lens-body">' + d.future + '</div></div>' +
    '<div class="cov-modal-deeper">' +
    '<button class="cov-deeper-btn" onclick="closeCovModal();showTab(\'arguments\')">See the 12 Key Arguments →</button>' +
    '<button class="cov-deeper-btn" onclick="closeCovModal();showTab(\'millennial\')">The Millennial Proof →</button>' +
    '<button class="cov-deeper-btn" onclick="closeCovModal();showTab(\'language\')">Original Language Analysis →</button>' +
    (key==='renewed'?'<button class="cov-deeper-btn" style="border-color:var(--gold);color:var(--gold);" onclick="closeCovModal();showTab(\'covenants\');setTimeout(function(){var el=document.getElementById(\'gentile-section\');if(el)el.scrollIntoView({behavior:\'smooth\',block:\'start\'});},300)">Who Is the Gentile Believer? The Grafting In →</button>':'') +
    '</div>';
  document.getElementById('covModal').classList.add('open');
}
function closeCovModal() { document.getElementById('covModal').classList.remove('open'); }

